WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.317
Hello.

2
00:00:04.907 --> 00:00:08.100
My name is Mohammad Ali Jaffry.

3
00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.037
I am an architect.

4
00:00:12.627 --> 00:00:15.750
Ten years of architectural experience.

5
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.907
Episode 2.

6
00:00:20.497 --> 00:00:21.937
Types of software.

7
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.231
Quick quiz.

8
00:00:35.821 --> 00:00:38.712
What kind of software is Kubernetes?

9
00:00:40.301 --> 00:00:50.425
Here is the answer the chapter gives: it is system software, delivered as a platform, and it is not itself a microservice.

10
00:00:52.015 --> 00:00:58.784
Three answers to one question — because the question ‘what kind of software is this’ is really four questions in disguise.

11
00:00:59.884 --> 00:01:03.402
There is no single list of software types.

12
00:01:04.992 --> 00:01:08.150
A useful classification says which question it is answering.

13
00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.633
Four questions cover what this research needs.

14
00:01:14.223 --> 00:01:17.114
What job does the program do beside the hardware?

15
00:01:18.474 --> 00:01:20.738
What kind of problem is it built to solve?

16
00:01:22.098 --> 00:01:24.362
How does a customer receive it?

17
00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:28.994
And how is a business application structured?

18
00:01:30.584 --> 00:01:34.682
One program occupies one place on each cut.

19
00:01:36.232 --> 00:01:38.159
This is the everyday cut.

20
00:01:39.749 --> 00:01:44.649
System software operates the machine and makes it usable by other programs.

21
00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:50.143
It schedules processes, stores bytes, and moves packets.

22
00:01:51.733 --> 00:01:53.648
An operating system.

23
00:01:55.238 --> 00:01:57.432
A container runtime.

24
00:01:59.022 --> 00:02:01.971
Kubernetes, as the deployment platform.

25
00:02:03.561 --> 00:02:05.593
A database engine.

26
00:02:07.183 --> 00:02:15.925
Middleware sits between system software and the application, and carries messages, requests, and identity from one program to another.

27
00:02:17.065 --> 00:02:18.819
A message broker.

28
00:02:20.408 --> 00:02:21.976
An API gateway.

29
00:02:23.566 --> 00:02:25.017
A service mesh.

30
00:02:26.607 --> 00:02:28.732
The PaaS router.

31
00:02:30.322 --> 00:02:34.768
Application software does a job a person or another business system asked for.

32
00:02:35.908 --> 00:02:41.365
The order service, the delivery service, and the SaaS product the tenant uses.

33
00:02:42.505 --> 00:02:46.777
Development software builds, tests, and releases the other three.

34
00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:53.966
Compilers, buildpacks, pipelines, test runners, and contract-test tools.

35
00:02:55.516 --> 00:03:08.891
Roger Pressman’s textbook groups programs by the problem they solve: system software, application software, engineering and scientific, embedded, product-line, web applications, and artificial-intelligence software.

36
00:03:10.031 --> 00:03:12.840
Keep that last one in mind.

37
00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.335
It is going to matter.

38
00:03:17.884 --> 00:03:25.280
The third cut is the one the US standards body NIST made for cloud computing, plus the older packaged form.

39
00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:30.100
On-premises: a copy you install and operate.

40
00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:35.429
Infrastructure as a service: machines you fill.

41
00:03:37.019 --> 00:03:40.804
Platform as a service: your application, on their runtime.

42
00:03:41.944 --> 00:03:45.508
Software as a service: a login and your data.

43
00:03:46.648 --> 00:03:52.209
Here is the sentence to keep: the delivery type does not change the problem type.

44
00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:57.921
It changes who patches the servers and who onboards the next customer.

45
00:03:59.021 --> 00:04:07.310
The fourth cut is the subject of the whole series: monolith, modular monolith, microservices, self-contained systems, serverless functions.

46
00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.737
And every cell in this grid is real.

47
00:04:12.327 --> 00:04:14.649
A monolith can be SaaS.

48
00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:20.512
A microservice suite can be packaged and run on a company’s own machines.

49
00:04:21.652 --> 00:04:26.470
Kubernetes and a PaaS host whichever structure the team chose.

50
00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.478
They are not themselves one of these structures.

51
00:04:32.027 --> 00:04:33.305
Now the payoff.

52
00:04:34.895 --> 00:04:40.920
Pressman’s artificial-intelligence category is software whose job is recognition, search, or learned judgment.

53
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.450
The research question is different, and easy to blur with it.

54
00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:51.850
An AI feature inside the order service is Pressman’s category living inside an application.

55
00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:58.539
An AI tool that proposes service boundaries is development software acting on the structure cut.

56
00:04:59.679 --> 00:05:01.386
Two different things.

57
00:05:02.976 --> 00:05:06.308
Naming which cut moved is the discipline of the later chapters.

58
00:05:07.408 --> 00:05:09.567
Three things to carry away.

59
00:05:11.157 --> 00:05:11.807
One.

60
00:05:13.397 --> 00:05:17.171
Four cuts, four questions; one program sits in one cell of each.

61
00:05:18.311 --> 00:05:18.926
Two.

62
00:05:20.516 --> 00:05:27.819
Kubernetes and a PaaS are system software that hosts a structure; they are not a structure.

63
00:05:29.409 --> 00:05:30.128
Three.

64
00:05:31.718 --> 00:05:36.734
The research is about the structure cut — not about Pressman’s AI category.

65
00:05:38.284 --> 00:05:40.025
Next: the history.

66
00:05:41.615 --> 00:05:45.911
Nobody invented microservices on a given day — so where did they come from?
